---
import Callout from "../../guide/Callout.astro";
import GuidePage from "../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../guide/Path.astro";
import Task from "../../guide/Task.astro";
import { leavesSite, REPOSITORY, sitePath } from "../../site/links.ts";
---

<GuidePage slug="troubleshooting" lead="The problems people run into most, and what fixes them.">
  <Task id="verify" title="A source asks you to prove you're human">
    <p>Sites behind Cloudflare and similar checks fail with <strong>Failed to bypass Cloudflare</strong> or an HTTP error such as 403.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the title, or the source's error screen, and tap <strong>WebView</strong> (or <strong>Open in WebView</strong>).</li>
      <li>
        Complete the check in the page that opens, then go back. Tap <strong>Retry</strong> on the source's error screen, or pull down on
        the title to refresh it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Still failing? Under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, tap <strong>Clear cookies</strong>, or <strong>Clear WebView data</strong>, and
        try again.
      </li>
      <li>
        Some sites block Katari's browser signature. Changing <strong>Default user agent string</strong> on the same screen, then restarting
        Katari, can help.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>Keep Android System WebView up to date. An old one fails these checks more often.</p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="source-errors" title="A source shows an error">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>HTTP 429</strong>: the site is limiting how often you ask. Wait a while, and check fewer titles at once.</li>
      <li>Other <strong>HTTP</strong> codes: open the site in <strong>WebView</strong> to see what it says.</li>
      <li><strong>No Internet connection</strong> or <strong>Couldn't reach</strong> the site: check the connection, or try <strong>DNS over HTTPS (DoH)</strong> under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>.</li>
      <li><strong>Source not found</strong>: its extension is missing. Install it again from your extension store.</li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="extensions" title="An extension doesn't show up">
    <p>Under <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path>, extensions that can't be used are listed under <strong>Not loaded</strong>:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shown as</th> <th>What to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Untrusted</strong></td> <td>Tap the shield and <strong>Trust</strong> if you trust where it came from.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Filtered</strong></td> <td>Its content warning is one you didn't allow. Change <strong>Allowed content warnings</strong> under <Path>Settings › Browse</Path>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Conflict</strong></td> <td>Another installed extension provides the same sources. See below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Unsupported</strong></td> <td>It finds episodes as torrents, which Katari can't play. Use another extension.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No label</td> <td>Tap the info button for the reason. Usually updating the extension or Katari helps. When it says the file couldn't be read, uninstall it and install it again.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Katari extensions numbered below 3.0, and Mihon extensions made for an old extension library, are no longer loaded. Install
      their current version from your extension store.
    </p>
    <p>
      An installed extension marked <strong>Orphaned</strong> still works, but no store you've added lists it any more, so it gets no
      updates. Uninstall it, or add its store again.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Conflict</strong> also shows on a working extension when some of its sources come from another one. The extension's page lists
      them under <strong>Provided by other extensions</strong>; tap <strong>Use this one</strong> beside a source to take it over.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="video" title="A video won't play">
    <p>The player says what went wrong and offers what can help. The most common:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The source couldn't load this episode</strong> or <strong>The video server refused the stream</strong>: tap
        <strong>Open in WebView</strong> and pass the site's check, then <strong>Retry</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A server that doesn't respond: the player tries the next one by itself. If you picked the server, tap <strong>Try another
        server</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>This video needs the mpv add-on</strong>: tap <strong>Install add-on</strong>, or pick another source. See
        <a href={sitePath("guide/video/#mpv-addon")}>the mpv add-on</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The built-in player can't play it. mpv might.</strong> or the reverse: tap <strong>Try mpv</strong> or
        <strong>Try built-in</strong>. <strong>Neither player could play it</strong> needs another stream or quality.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Only offered as a torrent</strong>: Katari doesn't play torrents. Pick another dub or server.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Under <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player</Path>, the mpv add-on's row says when something is wrong with it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>This Katari version needs a newer add-on</strong>: tap <strong>Update</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Signed with another key than Katari, so Katari can't use it</strong>: the add-on came from somewhere else. Remove it with the
        delete button, then install it from the same screen.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No mpv add-on for this version of Katari has been published.</strong>: wait for one, or update Katari.</li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="background" title="Updates or backups stop in the background">
    <p>
      Android puts apps to sleep to save battery, and some phone makers are much stricter about it. Under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, tap
      <strong>Disable battery optimization</strong>, then follow the steps for your phone on <a href="https://dontkillmyapp.com" {...leavesSite()}>Don't kill my app!</a>
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="models" title="Text recognition models don't download">
    <p>
      <strong>Waiting for a non-metered network</strong> means the download was approved for Wi-Fi only. Connect to Wi-Fi, or start it
      again and tick <strong>Allow download over mobile data</strong>. <strong>Not enough storage</strong> needs space freed first. See
      <a href={sitePath("guide/translation/#text-recognition")}>Translation</a>.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="app-update" title="An app update won't install">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>This update is signed with a different key than the installed app</strong>: the Katari you have was built by someone else,
        and Android won't replace it. <a href={sitePath("guide/backups-and-storage/#backup")}>Back up</a>, uninstall it, install Katari from
        the <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/releases/latest`} {...leavesSite()}>release page</a>, and restore the backup.
      </li>
      <li><strong>GitHub is limiting update checks from this network</strong>: try again later.</li>
      <li><strong>There isn't enough free storage to install the update</strong>: free some space and try again.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout label="Two apps">
      <p>
        Katari and Katari FOSS are separate apps with separate libraries. Installing one doesn't update or replace the other; move your
        library between them with a <a href={sitePath("guide/backups-and-storage/#backup")}>backup</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="report" title="Report a problem">
    <ol>
      <li>Under <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path>, tap <strong>Share crash logs</strong> and save or send the file from the share sheet.</li>
      <li>In <Path>Settings › About</Path>, tap <strong>Version</strong> to copy the version details.</li>
      <li>Open an <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/issues`} {...leavesSite()}>issue on GitHub</a> with both, and the steps that lead to the problem.</li>
    </ol>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
